/**
  @page examples-pdes-surfacemesh Solving PDEs on surface meshes
  @brief Solve reaction–diffusion on a triangulated unit sphere.

  A surface mesh has two-dimensional cells whose coordinates lie in
  three-dimensional space. This example builds a closed spherical surface and
  solves a scalar reaction–diffusion equation on it. The same variational syntax
  as in a planar problem uses tangential gradients and surface measure.

  @tableofcontents
  @m_footernavigation

  @section examples-pdes-surface-intro The mathematical problem

  Let @f$\Gamma=\{x\in\mathbb R^3:|x|=1\}@f$ be the unit sphere. Solve
  @f[
    -\Delta_\Gamma u+u=f,\qquad f(x,y,z)=7xy.
  @f]
  The operator @f$\Delta_\Gamma@f$ is the Laplace–Beltrami operator. Since
  @f$-\Delta_\Gamma(xy)=6xy@f$ on the unit sphere, the exact smooth-surface
  solution is @f$u_{\mathrm{ex}}=xy@f$.

  Multiplying by @f$v\in H^1(\Gamma)@f$ and integrating over the closed surface
  gives
  @f[
    \int_\Gamma\nabla_\Gamma u\cdot\nabla_\Gamma v\,ds
       +\int_\Gamma uv\,ds=\int_\Gamma fv\,ds.
  @f]
  There is no exterior boundary on which to prescribe a boundary condition.
  The positive reaction term removes the constant kernel of the surface Laplacian.

  @section examples-pdes-surface-mesh 1. Build the surface geometry

  A tetrahedral cube is centered at the origin. Its boundary is extracted with
  `skin()`, then each surface vertex is projected radially onto the unit sphere.

  @snippet PDEs/SurfaceMesh.cpp surface-mesh

  The default result has 602 vertices and 1200 triangular facets. Its topological
  dimension is two and its embedding dimension is three. The flat facets define a polyhedral approximation @f$\Gamma_h@f$ to the sphere. Interior points of a facet generally lie inside the sphere.

  @section examples-pdes-surface-problem 2. Define the surface problem

  `P1` defines a continuous field affine on each facet. Rodin uses the
  rectangular geometric Jacobian to evaluate the tangential gradient and the
  surface measure used by the weak form.

  @snippet PDEs/SurfaceMesh.cpp surface-form

  The formula is assembled on @f$\Gamma_h@f$, with the source evaluated at
  its integration points. Comparing with @f$xy@f$ therefore includes both
  finite element error and geometric approximation error. Higher geometric accuracy requires refinement or a curved representation of the
  mesh.

  @section examples-pdes-surface-output 3. Solve and visualize the field

  The stiffness plus reaction matrix is symmetric positive definite, so
  conjugate gradients is appropriate. The mesh and scalar field are exported
  together after convergence is checked.

  @snippet PDEs/SurfaceMesh.cpp surface-solve

  After the error and residual checks below pass, export the field.

  @snippet PDEs/SurfaceMesh.cpp surface-export

  @image html PDEs/SurfaceMesh.png "Computed reaction–diffusion field on the triangulated sphere. Color represents the scalar field on the original triangulated geometry." width=720px

  Open `SurfaceMesh.xdmf` in ParaView, color by **u**, and select **Surface With
  Edges** to inspect the geometry. Positive and negative lobes reflect the sign
  of @f$xy@f$. The surface is displayed in its three-dimensional embedding.

  @section examples-pdes-surface-error 4. Check geometry and field refinement

  The executable records the relative algebraic residual and the field error
  @f[
    e_h=\frac{\lVert u_h-xy\rVert_{L^2(\Gamma_h)}}
                 {\lVert xy\rVert_{L^2(\Gamma_h)}}.
  @f]
  Here @f$xy@f$ is the ambient polynomial evaluated on each flat facet.
  The norm is therefore taken on the actual mesh surface, using that
  extension of the smooth-sphere solution. It includes the effect of both
  field discretization and approximate geometry. Sixth-order triangle
  quadrature evaluates the squared-error polynomial.

  @snippet PDEs/SurfaceMesh.cpp surface-error

  An optional positive integer argument selects the number of background
  vertices per axis, with at least three required. The following checked
  sequence halves the original cube-grid spacing at each step:

  | Vertices per axis | Surface vertices | Triangles | Surface area | Relative field error |
  | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
  | 6 | 152 | 300 | @f$12.27686@f$ | @f$0.0940273@f$ |
  | 11 | 602 | 1200 | @f$12.49211@f$ | @f$0.0251292@f$ |
  | 21 | 2402 | 4800 | @f$12.54768@f$ | @f$0.00640798@f$ |

  @image html SurfaceMeshConvergence.png "Measured relative field error and relative surface-area error against the analytic sphere area. The dashed curve is a quadratic reference anchored at the finest field error."

  The field errors decrease by factors @f$3.74@f$ and @f$3.92@f$,
  approaching quadratic behavior on this sequence. The area approaches
  @f$4\pi@f$. Every assembled residual is below @f$6.1\times10^{-11}@f$
  after normalization by @f$\max(1,\lVert b\rVert_2)@f$.

  Run the three cases one at a time in separate directories to retain their
  XDMF/HDF5 output and `SurfaceMesh.csv` statistics. The executable checks
  solver success, finite measurements and a relative residual below
  @f$10^{-9}@f$ before writing the field.

  @section examples-pdes-surface-full Complete program

  The program generates its mesh at startup.

  See @ref PDEs/SurfaceMesh.cpp "SurfaceMesh.cpp", the
  @ref guides-meshes-creation "mesh creation guide", and the
  @ref guides-io "I/O guide" for related operations.

  @example PDEs/SurfaceMesh.cpp @m_examplenavigation{examples-pdes-surfacemesh,PDEs/} @m_footernavigation
*/
